.TH MU2EDAQ-TOPOLOGY.YAML 5 "2026-09-18" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
topology.yaml \- Mu2e DAQ node inventory for the power-recovery tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I config/topology.yaml
.SH DESCRIPTION
The node inventory: which machines exist, where they are, which networks they
are on, what class they belong to, and which of them must never be powered off.
.PP
The per-network entry syntax is deliberately identical to
.I mu2edaq-operations/scripts/nodes_config.yaml,
so node lists can be copied between the two files without translation. That
repository remains the authoritative inventory; this file adds the CIDRs, the
gateways, the host classes and the protection list that a recovery needs.
.SH TOP-LEVEL KEYS
.TP
.I domain
DNS domain appended to generated hostnames. Default
.IR fnal.gov .
.TP
.I default_prefix
Site prefix for generated hostnames. Default
.IR mu2e .
.TP
.IR ipmi_suffix ", " data_suffix
How a lab hostname maps to its BMC and data-network names. Defaults
.IR \-ipmi " and " \-data ,
so the BMC of
.I mu2e-trk-01.fnal.gov
is
.IR mu2e-trk-01-ipmi.fnal.gov .
.TP
.I protected
Hosts for which destructive IPMI verbs are refused, regardless of
.BR \-\-execute .
Powering any of these down would cut the operator off from the cluster they are
recovering, or stop the NFS server the rest of it mounts from. Matches on the
short name, so a fully-qualified entry protects the short form too.
.TP
.I roles
Ordered list of
.RI { pattern ", " class }
mappings. The first regular expression matching a host's short name assigns its
class; a host matching nothing lands in
.BR other .
Classes group the report tables and select a check profile.
.IP
The shipped classes are
.BR gateway ", " manager ", " datalogger ", " dcs ", " cfo ", " tracker ,
.BR calorimeter ", " crv ", " stm ", " extmon ", " worker " and " teststand .
A class is only used to pick a profile when
.BR mu2edaq-checks.yaml (5)
defines a profile of the same name; the detector classes do not have one, so in
phase 1 they fall back to the
.B other
profile and it is phase 2 that applies
.B readout
to them, through the stage's
.I checks
key. Adding a class here without adding a profile of the same name is
therefore silently a no-op for phase 1.
.TP
.I locations
The sites, described below.
.SH LOCATIONS
Each location holds:
.TP
.I label
Short human name, used as the heading of the location's report tables. It
falls back to the upper-cased location key when absent.
.TP
.I description
Longer human name. Recorded for the reader of this file only \(em nothing
reads it.
.TP
.I aliases
Alternative spellings accepted on the command line. The teststand accepts
.BR heerc .
Hyphens are ignored, so
.B mc-2
and
.B mc2
are the same location.
.TP
.I gateways
Jump hosts used to reach this location, in preference order.
.TP
.I subnets
Network name to CIDR. These are what
.I net.interfaces
matches an address against, which is why the check is independent of interface
naming \(em it looks for an address in the expected subnet, not for
.IR eno1 .
.TP
.I networks
Network name to entry list: which hosts are on each network, under the name
used for that network's
.I subnets
entry.
.IP
Two of the names are not IP networks.
.B pcie
is a card inventory \(em membership means "this host carries a DTC or CFO
card", and
.IR pcie.devices " and " pcie.driver
report
.B skip
on a host that is not in it rather than failing. The teststand's
.B ctrl
network has no
.I subnets
entry, and a network with no CIDR recorded is not interface-checked.
.IP
.I net.interfaces
looks only at networks the node is in
.I and
for which a CIDR is recorded, and skips
.BR ipmi " and " pcie
in any case: a BMC address does not belong to the host's own stack, and
.B pcie
is not an address at all.
.SH ENTRY SYNTAX
Each entry in a network's list is either a fully-qualified hostname, used
verbatim, or a mapping describing a run of numbered hosts:
.TP
.IR start ", " end
First and last node number, inclusive.
.TP
.I category
Subsystem label inserted between the prefix and the number, giving
.IR <prefix>-<category>-<NN><suffix>.<domain> .
Omit it for hosts whose prefix already encodes the type, giving
.IR <prefix><NN><suffix>.<domain> .
.TP
.I prefix
Overrides
.IR default_prefix .
.TP
.I suffix
Appended after the number, e.g.
.IR \-data ", " \-ipmi .
.TP
.I excludes
Node numbers to skip. This is how a decommissioned machine, or one with no BMC,
is kept out of a list.
.TP
.I fmt
printf-style format for the number. Default
.BR %02d .
.SH THE CURRENT INVENTORY
65 hosts across three locations, as shipped.
.BR "mu2e-node-inventory \-\-json"
prints what this file actually expands to, which is the only answer that
cannot go stale.
.TP
.B mc2
MC-2, the primary DAQ room. Gateways
.IR mu2egateway01 " and " mu2egateway02 .
Subnets: lab 131.225.245.0/24, data 10.226.9.0/24, IPMI 192.168.157.0/24, mgmt
131.225.245.0/24. 48 hosts, on five networks: lab 48, data 42, ipmi 37, pcie
36, mgmt 5.
.IP
The lists are not parallel, and must not be assumed to be. The
.B ipmi
list excludes tracker chassis 5\(en8, 10 and 13 and calorimeter chassis 8\(en10
and 12, which have no BMC entry; the
.B data
list omits the gateways, the manager and the DCS hosts altogether; the
.B pcie
list starts the STM range at 2, because only
.I mu2e-stm-02
carries a card; and
.I mu2e-dcs-03
appears on
.BR lab " and " mgmt
only. A host on the lab network therefore does not necessarily have a BMC this
tool can reach, or a card for
.I pcie.devices
to look for.
.TP
.B mc1
MC-1.
.B Both of its node lists are empty.
MC-1 is not carried in the upstream
.I nodes_config.yaml,
so no inventory could be imported. What is recorded is its lab subnet
(131.225.246.0/24), the IPMI subnet it shares with MC-2 (192.168.157.0/24), the
absence of a data network, and the two MC-2 gateways, which is how it is
reached until it has its own. Add hostnames to
.I locations.mc1.networks
and every phase picks MC-1 up with no code change; until then the tools report
it as having no nodes configured rather than as healthy.
.TP
.B teststand
The Helen Edwards Engineering Center (HEERC) test stand, also accepted as
.BR heerc .
Gateway
.IR mu2edaq-gateway .
Subnets: lab 131.225.237.0/24, data 10.226.9.0/24, IPMI 192.168.150.0/24.
Networks:
.BR ctrl ", " lab ", " ipmi ", " data " and " pcie .
Hosts are
.IR mu2edaqNN ,
with three overlapping exclusion sets \(em decommissioned or offline nodes
(1, 3, 5, 6, 12, 15\(en17), nodes present but outside the HEERC cluster
(2, 8, 9, 18\(en21, 23, 24), and nodes with no DTC card (13, 14) \(em combined
differently per network. 17 hosts, counting the gateway: lab 17, pcie 14, ctrl
8, ipmi 8, data 7.
.SH EXAMPLES
Add a tracker node and its interfaces:
.RS
.EX
locations:
  mc2:
    networks:
      lab:
        \- {category: trk, start: 1, end: 19}
      ipmi:
        \- {category: trk, start: 1, end: 19, suffix: \-ipmi,
           excludes: [5, 6, 7, 8, 10, 13]}
      data:
        \- {category: trk, start: 1, end: 19, suffix: \-data}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check what the change produced:
.RS
.EX
mu2e-node-inventory \-l mc2 \-c tracker
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2e-node-inventory (1),
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1),
.BR mu2edaq-power-sequence.yaml (5),
.BR mu2edaq-checks.yaml (5)
